chapters.content:
Her parents sent both girls to Pencarrow College, the comprehensive in Penzance where Olivia had gone thirty years earlier. Kayleigh was never an easy pupil to place on an academic grid. She was brilliant at English, passable at Geography (mainly because she liked drawing coastlines), and aggressively indifferent to anything involving quadratic equations. Her reports consistently used words like 'engaged', 'distracted', 'ferocious when interested', and 'a disruptive influence during silent study'.

Three of her favourite school memories with Leah were:
First, Year 9, when they convinced the supply French teacher that 'le Cornish pasty' was a recognised AOC foodstuff with its own grammar;
Second, the December afternoon when the sea wall breached at high tide and the whole school was evacuated through the back gates, Kayleigh and Leah walking the three miles back along the cliff path with salt crusting on their blazers and laughing so hard they had stitches;
And third, the battle of the bands in Year 11, which Kayleigh entered with a three-piece called The Lowen and lost to a sixth-form Coldplay covers outfit, which made her so incandescent she spent the whole summer holiday in the garage learning EVH tap solos at 180bpm until her fingertips bled into the fretboard.

She came out to Leah first, at fifteen, sitting on the granite jetty at Mousehole with two salt-vinegar bags of chips between them. Leah looked up, chewed once, and said: 'Yeah, obviously. Did you think that was a surprise?' Olivia was second, a week later over scrambled eggs. Her mother set down the spatula, kissed the crown of her head, and said, 'Tea, darling? Or did you want a whole conversation about it?' Kayleigh still says that five-second non-event was the greatest single act of parenting she ever received.
